{
  "dom_id": "step-e6231",
  "name": "Example: Dividing a Real Number by a Complex Number",
  "problem": "Express $\\dfrac{4}{-1+2\\textrm{i} }$ in the form $a+\\textrm{i}b.$",
  "worked_solution": "The denominator is $-1+2\\textrm{i}$ and its conjugate is $-1-2\\textrm{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-1-2\\textrm{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {4} {-1 +2\\textrm{i}} \n&=\\dfrac {4} {-1 +2 \\textrm{i}} \\cdot \\dfrac {-1 -2 \\textrm{i}} {-1-2\\textrm{i}}  \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {-4 - 8 \\textrm{i} } {(-1)^2 - (2\\textrm{i})^2}  \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {-4 - 8 \\textrm{i} } {1 - 4\\textrm{i}^2}  \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {-4 - 8 \\textrm{i} } {1 - 4(-1)}  \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {-4 - 8 \\textrm{i} } {5} \n\t}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b \\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {-4 - 8 \\textrm{i} } {5} =\n\t\t-\\dfrac{4} {5} - \\dfrac {8} {5} \\textrm{i}\t\n\t}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
